LLM_output:
def count_vowels(text):
    count = 0
    for char in text.lower():
        if char in "aeiou":
            count += 1
    return count

Converting the string to lowercase makes the count ignore letter case.

```python
def count_vowels(text):
    vowels = set("aeiou")
    return sum(1 for ch in text.lower() if ch in vowels)
```

def remove_duplicates(items):
    seen = set()
    result = []
    for item in items:
        if item not in seen:
            seen.add(item)
            result.append(item)
    return result

The set tracks values already seen, and each value is added to the result only on its first occurrence, preserving order.

def remove_duplicates(lst):
    seen = set()
    out = []
    for x in lst:
        if x not in seen:
            seen.add(x)
            out.append(x)
    return out

The default list is created only once when the function is defined, so every call without an items argument shares and modifies the same list. Use None as the default instead:
def add_item(item, items=None):
    if items is None:
        items = []
    items.append(item)
    return items

The default list is created once and shared across calls. Fix by using None:
def add_item(item, items=None):
    if items is None:
        items = []
    items.append(item)
    return items
